# Deploy — Azure AKS (Terraform + Helm)

This project was scaffolded with the **Azure AKS** IaC target. Infrastructure is
described with Terraform and the application is released with Helm. Every command
is scoped per environment via `--env`, which is **required** (no default) — run a
command without it to list the environments discovered under
`iac/terraform/environments/`. Add a new one with `dude iac new-env --env <name>`.
State lives in an Azure Storage blob container, so the same commands run locally
and in CI.

!!! info "The IaC runner (Docker)"
    `dude iac *` runs terraform / kubectl / helm / k9s inside a pinned container
    built from `iac/runner/Dockerfile` (yours to edit) — so you only need
    **Docker** and the `az` CLI installed, not the whole toolchain. Your
    `~/.azure` and `~/.kube` are mounted in, so your Azure login and the cluster
    context keep working. `dude iac login` stays on the host (it needs a browser).
    Set `DUDE_IAC_RUNNER=host` to use your local tools instead.

## What gets provisioned

| Layer | Resource | Notes |
|-------|----------|-------|
| Resource group | `{{projectName}}-<env>-rg` | everything for one environment lives in it, so teardown is total |
| Network | Virtual network + AKS subnet{{#if withPostgres}} + delegated database subnet{{/if}} | Azure CNI **overlay**, so pod IPs don't consume the subnet |
| Cluster | AKS control plane + autoscaling node pool | `Standard_D2as_v5` nodes, 2–4, Free control-plane tier |
| Registry | Azure Container Registry | `{{projectName}}-backend`, `{{projectName}}-frontend` — **shared** across envs, created once by `dude iac bootstrap` |
| Ingress | ingress-nginx + an Azure public IP with a DNS label | gives the app a real hostname with no domain of your own — see below |
| TLS *(optional)* | cert-manager + Let's Encrypt | needs a domain **you own**; see "HTTPS" below for why Azure's hostname can't have one |
{{#if withPostgres}}| Database | Azure Database for PostgreSQL Flexible Server | private (VNet-integrated, no public endpoint); password generated by Terraform, `DATABASE_URL` wired into Helm automatically by `dude iac ship` |
{{/if}}{{#if withRedis}}| Cache/broker | Redis (in-cluster) | broker/result backend for Celery |
{{/if}}| Release | Helm chart `iac/helm/app` | backend + frontend{{#if withCelery}} + Celery worker{{/if}}{{#if withCeleryBeat}} + Celery Beat{{/if}} |
| State backend | Storage account + `tfstate` blob container | created once by `dude iac bootstrap`; the blob lease **is** the state lock, so there is no lock table |

## Before you start

You need, on your machine:

- **Docker**, running.
- The **Azure CLI** (`az`) — `brew install azure-cli`, or see
  [Microsoft's install guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/cli/azure/install-azure-cli).
- An Azure subscription where your account can **create resource groups** and
  **assign roles** (the Contributor + User Access Administrator pair, or Owner).
  The role assignment is what lets the cluster pull from the registry; without it
  `dude iac apply` fails at `azurerm_role_assignment.acr_pull`.

## Deploy, in order

```bash
# 1. Sign in to Azure and confirm which subscription this environment uses
dude iac login --env dev

# 2. One-time per project: create the shared resource group, the Terraform state
#    storage account and the container registry, then wire them into the env
#    config. The prefix makes those names globally unique across Azure — use your
#    org slug, lowercase letters/digits only. You do NOT repeat this for more envs.
dude iac bootstrap --state-prefix <yourorg> --env dev --yes

# 3. Initialise Terraform against the remote backend
dude iac init --env dev

# 4. Provision the infrastructure (resource group, VNet, AKS, ingress-nginx{{#if withPostgres}}, database{{/if}})
#    Expect ~10 minutes: the cluster and the load balancer both take a while.
dude iac apply --env dev

# 5. Point kubectl at the new cluster
dude iac kubeconfig --env dev

# 6. Build, push and deploy the app in one step (build + push + helm upgrade --install).
#    The registry, image tag and public hostname are wired automatically — no need
#    to hand-edit values files.
dude iac ship --env dev
```

Then open the URL `dude iac output --env dev` prints as `app_url`:

```
http://{{projectName}}-dev.westeurope.cloudapp.azure.com/
```

Check what's running:

```bash
dude iac status --env dev          # helm release + pods
dude iac output --env dev          # cluster name, registry URLs, app URL…
```

## The public hostname

You do not need to own a domain. The ingress controller asks Azure for a public
IP **with a DNS label**, and Azure serves it at:

```
<dns_label>.<location>.cloudapp.azure.com
```

`dns_label` defaults to `{{projectName}}-<env>` and lives in the environment's
`terraform.tfvars`. It must be unique **within the region** — if `dude iac apply`
reports the name is taken, change it there and re-apply.

`dude iac deploy`/`ship` reads the resulting hostname from the Terraform
`app_host` output and sets it on the Ingress, so the chart's `ingress.host` is
left empty on purpose: it is a deploy-time fact, not a committed one.

## HTTPS

**The Azure-provided hostname is served over plain HTTP, and that is not a
configuration you can flip.** `cloudapp.azure.com` is not on the
[Public Suffix List](https://publicsuffix.org/), so Let's Encrypt treats every
certificate for it as a certificate for `azure.com` — one rate limit shared by
every Azure customer on earth, which is permanently exhausted. No free, trusted
certificate is obtainable for that name.

Two ways to get HTTPS:

1. **Point a domain you own at the ingress** (the supported path). Create a CNAME
   from your hostname to `<dns_label>.<location>.cloudapp.azure.com`, then set in
   `iac/terraform/environments/dev/terraform.tfvars`:

   ```hcl
   letsencrypt_email = "ops@example.com"
   ```

   `dude iac apply` installs cert-manager and a `letsencrypt-prod` ClusterIssuer.
   Then set the host and turn TLS on in `iac/helm/app/values-dev.yaml`:

   ```yaml
   ingress:
     host: app.example.com
     tls:
       enabled: true
       clusterIssuer: letsencrypt-prod
   ```

   and `dude iac deploy --env dev`. The certificate is issued over HTTP-01
   through the same ingress, so nothing else needs opening.

2. **Put a fronting service with its own managed certificate in front** — Azure
   Front Door Standard serves `*.azurefd.net` with a Microsoft-issued
   certificate and needs no domain. It is not provisioned by this scaffold and
   carries a monthly base cost.

Until then, treat the HTTP URL as what it is: fine for a demo or an internal
review, not for anything handling real credentials.

## Add another environment (staging, prod, …)

Environments are independent infrastructure (their own resource group, cluster{{#if withPostgres}},
database{{/if}}) that **share one state backend and one container registry** — they
differ only by their Terraform state `key`. So adding `staging` does **not**
require another `bootstrap`.

```bash
# 1. Scaffold environments/staging/ by copying an existing env (default: dev).
#    Rewrites `environment` and `dns_label` for the new env, and copies its
#    gitignored helm/app/values-<from>.yaml → values-staging.yaml.
dude iac new-env --env staging            # or --from prod to copy a different one

# 2. Review the new env's settings in
#    iac/terraform/environments/staging/terraform.tfvars:
#      dns_label    = "{{projectName}}-staging"   # its own hostname
#      node_vm_size / node_max_count / db_sku_name / … to taste
#    …and iac/helm/app/values-staging.yaml (replicas, autoscaling, secrets, …).

# 3. Initialise and provision — same flow as the first env, minus bootstrap
#    (the state backend + registry already exist).
dude iac login      --env staging
dude iac init       --env staging
dude iac apply      --env staging
dude iac kubeconfig --env staging
dude iac ship       --env staging
```

> **Promoting an image.** Because the registry is shared, you can deploy the exact
> image already built for one env to another instead of rebuilding:
> `dude iac deploy --env staging --tag <sha>` (use the tag from
> `dude iac status --env dev`).

## Image tags

`dude iac build/push/ship` derive the tag from your git state:

| Working tree | Tag |
|--------------|-----|
| clean | `<git-short-sha>` |
| uncommitted changes | `<git-short-sha>-dirty-<hash-of-the-diff>` |

The `-dirty-<hash>` part is what makes iterating on a branch work: every edit
changes the digest, so every `dude iac ship` publishes a distinguishable image.

Pushing a tag that already exists **overwrites** it — Azure Container Registry on
the Basic tier does not enforce tag immutability — so `dude iac push`/`ship` warn
before doing it. If you are promoting a build rather than making a new one, use
`dude iac deploy --env <env> --tag <tag>`: the image is already in the registry
and there is nothing to rebuild.

## Ad-hoc work with `dude iac shell`

When you need to run commands directly against an environment — inspect state,
force-unlock a Terraform lock, debug a failing pod, or run a one-off Helm
operation — drop into the runner:

```bash
dude iac shell --env dev
```

This opens an interactive shell inside the Docker runner with the project mounted
at `/work`, the env's subscription exported, and `kubectl`/`helm`/`k9s` **already
pointed at this env's cluster and namespace** — so you don't inherit whatever
context your host's `kubectl` happened to be on. (If the cluster isn't reachable
yet, it falls back to your mounted `~/.kube`.) The full toolchain is available:

| Tool | Example |
|------|---------|
| `terraform` | `terraform state list` · `terraform force-unlock <id>` |
| `kubectl` | `kubectl get pods -n dev` · `kubectl logs <pod> -n dev -f` · `kubectl describe ingress -n dev` |
| `helm` | `helm get values {{projectName}} -n dev` · `helm history {{projectName}} -n dev` |
| `k9s` | `k9s` — full interactive cluster UI |
| `az` | `az aks list -o table` · `az acr repository list -n <registry>` |

The project root is `/work` — relative paths in Terraform and Helm configs
resolve correctly from there. Type `exit` (or Ctrl-D) to leave the runner.

## Tear down

```bash
dude iac destroy --env staging        # destroy non-last envs first
dude iac destroy --env dev            # the last env also removes the shared backend + registry
```

For each environment, `destroy` uninstalls the Helm release (so the ingress
controller releases the public IP cleanly), then runs `terraform destroy`, which
removes the environment's whole resource group. It then re-checks that the group
is really gone, because Azure reporting a successful delete while the group
lingers — a deletion still in flight, or a resource lock — is the case that keeps
costing money quietly.

The **shared bootstrap** — the state storage account and the container registry —
is torn down **only when no other environment is still provisioned** (i.e. when
you destroy the last one). "Still provisioned" is judged from each sibling's
remote Terraform state, not its folder on disk — so a leftover `staging/` folder
whose infrastructure you already destroyed won't keep the backend alive.
Destroying a non-last environment never touches another env's state or the shared
registry. Pass `--keep-backend` to keep the bootstrap even when destroying the
last environment.

See `iac/README.md` for the full command reference and flags.
